pan class="enc-snip">Italicus was a king of the Cherusci in the 1st century CE, a son of Arminius' brother Flavus, who had stayed loyal to Rome.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/italiotes"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/italiotes.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Italiotes</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Italiotes were the Greeks of southern Italy, Magna Graecia, as distinct from the Siceliotes of Sicily.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/italo-balbo"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/italo-balbo.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Italo Balbo</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Italo Balbo was an Italian Fascist leader and aviator, one of the organisers of the March on Rome in 1922.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/italo-turkish-war"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/italo-turkish-war.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Italo-Turkish War</span><span class="enc-type">War · 1911–1912</span><span class="enc-snip">The Italo-Turkish War of 1911–1912 was fought between Italy and the Ottoman Empire.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/italy"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/italy.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Italy</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Italy is a peninsula and country in southern Europe, the homeland of the Roman Empire.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/itchan-kala"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/itchan-kala.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Itchan Kala</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Itchan Kala is the walled inner town of Khiva in Uzbekistan, the historic core of the city and the seat of the khans of Khiva.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ithaca"><span class="enc-name">Ithaca</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Ithaca is a small Ionian island off the west coast of Greece, between Cephalonia and the mainland, famed as the home of Odysseus in Homer's Odyssey.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ithaque-department"><span class="enc-name">Ithaque (department)</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Ithaque was a French department created in 1797 from the central Ionian Islands, formerly Venetian, after the Treaty of Campo Formio.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/itjtawy"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/itjtawy.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Itjtawy</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Itjtawy, short for Amenemhat-itj-tawy, 'Amenemhat seizes the Two Lands', was the royal capital of Egypt in the Twelfth and Thirteenth Dynasties.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ito-hirobumi"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ito-hirobumi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Itō Hirobumi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Itō Hirobumi (1841–1909) was a Japanese statesman from the Chōshū domain and the first prime minister of Japan, serving four times between 1885 and 1901.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/itsukushima"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/itsukushima.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Itsukushima</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Itsukushima, popularly known as Miyajima, is an island in Hiroshima Bay in the Inland Sea of Japan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ittobaal-of-byblos"><span class="enc-name">Ittobaal of Byblos</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ittobaal was a king of Byblos, the son of King Ahiram.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/itza-language"><span class="enc-name">Itza language</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">Itza is a Mayan language of the Yucatecan branch, spoken by the Itza Maya of the Petén lakes in northern Guatemala.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/itza-people"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/itza-people.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Itza people</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Itza are a Maya people of the Petén lakes in northern Guatemala.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/itzamna"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/itzamna.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Itzamna</span><span class="enc-type">Religion / idea</span><span class="enc-snip">Itzamna was one of the principal gods of the Maya, a creator deity associated with the sky, writing, knowledge and healing, often shown as an old man.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/itzamnaaj-bahlam-iii"><span class="enc-name">Itzamnaaj B'ahlam III</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Itzamnaaj B'ahlam III, known as Shield Jaguar the Great, was king of the Maya city of Yaxchilan from 681 to 742.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/itzamnaaj-kawiil"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/itzamnaaj-kawiil.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Itzamnaaj K'awiil</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Itzamnaaj K'awiil was the second known ruler of the Maya city of Dos Pilas in Guatemala, reigning from 698 to 726.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/itzcoatl"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/itzcoatl.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Itzcoatl</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Itzcoatl was the Mexica ruler who formed the alliance with Nezahualcoyotl of Texcoco and the city-state of Tlacopan against the Tepanec power of Azcapotzalco.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/iuput-high-priest"><span class="enc-name">Iuput (High Priest of Amun)</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Iuput was an Egyptian prince, a son of Shoshenq I, founder of the Twenty-second Dynasty.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/iuput-ii"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/iuput-ii.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Iuput II</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Iuput II was a king in the Nile Delta in the late 8th century BCE, usually located at Leontopolis and assigned to the Twenty-third Dynasty of Egypt.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ius-regale-montanorum"><span class="enc-name">Ius regale montanorum</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Ius regale montanorum was a mining code issued around 1300 by Wenceslaus II, king of Bohemia, for the silver mines of Kutná Hora.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/iuvavum"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/iuvavum.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Iuvavum</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Iuvavum was a Roman town in the province of Noricum on the Salzach, on the site of modern Salzburg in Austria.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ivailo"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ivailo.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ivailo</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ivailo was a swineherd who led a peasant rebellion in north-eastern Bulgaria in 1277 against Mongol raids and the failures of the nobility.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ivan-alexander-of-bulgaria"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ivan-alexander-of-bulgaria.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ivan Alexander of Bulgaria</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ivan Alexander was tsar of the Second Bulgarian Empire from 1331 to 1371.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ivan-andreyevich-of-mozhaysk"><span class="enc-name">Ivan Andreyevich of Mozhaysk</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ivan Andreyevich was prince of Mozhaysk, an appanage of the Grand Duchy of Moscow.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ivan-asen-i"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ivan-asen-i.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ivan Asen I</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ivan Asen I was the co-founder and first ruler of the Second Bulgarian Empire.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ivan-asen-ii"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ivan-asen-ii.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ivan Asen II</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ivan Asen II, son of Ivan Asen I, was tsar of Bulgaria from 1218 to 1241.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ivan-bunin"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ivan-bunin.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ivan Bunin</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ivan Bunin was a Russian writer, born in Voronezh in 1870 into an old noble family, who in 1933 became the first Russian writer awarded the Nobel Prize in Literature.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ivan-chistyakov"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ivan-chistyakov.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ivan Chistyakov</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ivan Chistyakov (1900–1979) was a Soviet colonel general who commanded armies at Stalingrad and Kursk during the Second World War.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ivan-crnojevic"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ivan-crnojevic.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ivan Crnojević</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ivan Crnojević was lord of Zeta, the forerunner of Montenegro, from 1465 to 1490.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ivan-dmitrievich-of-pereslavl"><span class="enc-name">Ivan Dmitrievich of Pereslavl</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ivan Dmitrievich was the last prince of Pereslavl-Zalessky, a son of Grand Prince Dmitry of Vladimir and grandson of Alexander Nevsky.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ivan-fyodorov"><span class="enc-name">Ivan Fyodorov</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ivan Fyodorov was a printer who, with Pyotr Mstislavets, produced the Apostol of 1564, the first precisely dated book printed in Moscow.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ivan-gundulic"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ivan-gundulic.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ivan Gundulić</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ivan Gundulić was a poet of the Republic of Ragusa (Dubrovnik) and the leading figure of Croatian Baroque literature.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ivan-i-of-moscow"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ivan-i-of-moscow.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ivan I of Moscow</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ivan I Danilovich, called Kalita ('moneybag'), was Prince of Moscow from 1325 and Grand Prince of Vladimir from 1328 until his death in 1340.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iii"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ivan-iii.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ivan III</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ivan III was Grand Prince of Moscow in the late fifteenth century, often called Ivan the Great.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iv-of-russia"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ivan-iv-of-russia.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ivan IV of Russia</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ivan IV, known as Ivan the Terrible, was the first ruler of Muscovy to be crowned tsar.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/ivan-ivanovich-of-ryazan"><span class="enc-name">Ivan Ivanovich of Ryazan</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ivan Ivanovich was the last grand prince of Ryazan, ruling from 1500.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ivan-mazepa"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ivan-mazepa.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="5